#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166716 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "3f266f66-1d79-5864-b497-1bc1b138c318" :question/math-academy-id "e-9499" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34e9e6f-2888-5f5e-8d46-214f1d392b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437366} {:answer/id #uuid "cb59301c-abc9-51ad-b0e3-c4ff391b8b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a34e9e6f-2888-5f5e-8d46-214f1d392b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437366} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0a4f31-7a33-5fef-82ec-37c61f532a12" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ad8b6d-306b-5c2f-9bc1-af6874eaf7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437368} {:answer/id #uuid "5afdf89b-d566-5135-9459-4a771ed47ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ad8b6d-306b-5c2f-9bc1-af6874eaf7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437368} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4bc42d9-4ade-5ab1-a4a6-2546001b1cbb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438713} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "970d8e6b-fc38-53e4-90d8-b02eee99b29d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437370} {:answer/id #uuid "790f5db3-f1d6-5dcd-bbeb-89e1313e0a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790f5db3-f1d6-5dcd-bbeb-89e1313e0a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437371} :answer-field/id #uuid "fabc5da5-1944-5cda-a505-51d0b2452429" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef2b9923-252b-570f-aaa0-557007eb97e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104570" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=x{e}^{xy}\\,i+y{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(xy+{y}^{2})\\,i+(xy+{x}^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=y{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=xy+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=xy+{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)$ and $Q=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5093965-6880-52c1-99d1-4b3fe1a5c833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438610} {:answer/id #uuid "46db8ede-df07-52d4-b08f-30a129c581d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5093965-6880-52c1-99d1-4b3fe1a5c833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438610} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8bbb111-e968-59e9-b2db-a637a9f734cc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8dccafb-21ba-532f-b09e-f2674009d0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438612} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6e66f0-53f1-5ecc-87b5-35c6b5302f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d6e66f0-53f1-5ecc-87b5-35c6b5302f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438613} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e852986-873b-5b33-96a5-b8861bc18ec2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439335} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de7ca771-fa92-5536-aebc-7cee5f961670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438614} {:answer/id #uuid "86c635ce-a4a6-5c29-a72f-7b9a4ef3b7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c635ce-a4a6-5c29-a72f-7b9a4ef3b7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438615} :answer-field/id #uuid "144d2103-4cd4-5e83-9245-447662f5adf6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "321a61eb-7345-5f73-a2c6-72200c42218f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81745" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=({x}^{2}+2y)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(y{e}^{x}+2)\\,i+({e}^{x}+4y)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=y\\,i+x\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q={x}^{2}+2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y{e}^{x}+2$ and $Q={e}^{x}+4y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y$ and $Q=x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5650cae6-4ebd-5fab-b924-a7307b4e29a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437372} {:answer/id #uuid "6993da07-2440-58cb-93b7-f9bd99118665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6993da07-2440-58cb-93b7-f9bd99118665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437373} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e37b67f-127d-5c2b-b4d2-843eb6fcfe09" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b38472-837a-578d-bc86-3da38e6632e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437374} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3efb19-98f5-5322-9115-86ca840350d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca3efb19-98f5-5322-9115-86ca840350d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437375} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18ab833-d485-51f2-bc95-50bb5a8ff3bb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5941ba21-ceee-5f9b-91b5-ac8cf172b28e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437376} {:answer/id #uuid "15d3424b-50ff-5a9b-a7ba-8a86b14dd910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15d3424b-50ff-5a9b-a7ba-8a86b14dd910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437377} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df86221-3e95-5646-bb83-ccb26044056c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ff94cca-f0c0-5b4f-9e96-5ccdc57e1837" :question/math-academy-id "q-104573" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(2x-y)\\,i-x\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-y$ and $Q=-x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q=-{e}^{x-y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65481b7b-861e-5720-a6dd-8d674d4da852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437384} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2c88c4-d301-5e5f-ada9-7861ac00371d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65481b7b-861e-5720-a6dd-8d674d4da852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437384} :answer-field/id #uuid "20435457-d381-5fe1-8f30-f9db1f097122" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "721521bd-d66b-56ad-b5bc-1f9a532924dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437386} {:answer/id #uuid "3b297167-e02b-58b6-a123-5866263ab82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b297167-e02b-58b6-a123-5866263ab82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437387} :answer-field/id #uuid "28606333-ca6f-5543-bf82-4a014fa763e8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438722} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53745e3-b275-5699-876b-c7e875b44edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437388} {:answer/id #uuid "53a6adf6-bbaa-5c44-b75e-33daf101bb6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53a6adf6-bbaa-5c44-b75e-33daf101bb6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437389} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd839e2a-5adc-5c75-b668-ba299a877b00" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aac0da30-e1e4-5293-838c-ddb161ffe326" :question/math-academy-id "q-104582" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative in their respective domains?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(2x-4y)\\,i+(4x-8y)\\,j$\n- **II.** $G={2}^{2x+y+1}\\,i+{2}^{2x+y}\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-4y$ and $Q=4x-8y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={2}^{2x+y+1}$ and $Q={2}^{2x+y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$ and $Q=\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71ea03ce-0bea-5a33-b768-531593cecfa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437710} {:answer/id #uuid "3e12d6c1-cf51-5a79-9824-a889289525f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71ea03ce-0bea-5a33-b768-531593cecfa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437710} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9705fa0-051d-5108-9f33-8e0b95bf6771" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71524ab4-c1fe-56a5-b9b0-5f1247ce034e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437712} {:answer/id #uuid "9809981f-9ddb-5aa8-be88-9c61eb732ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9809981f-9ddb-5aa8-be88-9c61eb732ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437713} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bc640c-07ec-54b1-ad03-d8c8b9f0a1f6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438885} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425e03bd-f65f-5c5d-a11e-3e1e0b0bfa52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437714} {:answer/id #uuid "6312d848-ea3c-5408-9f3b-a4a431e27732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6312d848-ea3c-5408-9f3b-a4a431e27732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437715} :answer-field/id #uuid "482a8231-c447-5ba1-b319-c14d7a80bd76" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc1786af-49ed-5f49-98af-90852a6f6e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-123984" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=3{e}^{3x+2y}\\,i+2{e}^{3x+2y}\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=3{e}^{3x+2y}$ and $Q=2{e}^{3x+2y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6{e}^{3x+2y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6{e}^{3x+2y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac5898e-ccd9-5404-8efc-311f58a28a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438598} {:answer/id #uuid "40d3c152-dad7-5945-a794-9fb168302c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40d3c152-dad7-5945-a794-9fb168302c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438599} :answer-field/id #uuid "6578c37d-0b49-5206-b245-6ec044a9e0dd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec262c2d-cd4c-5f8a-9d3f-9bbc45bf6356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438600} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d3825b-3dd0-5749-8394-e80247b12499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5d3825b-3dd0-5749-8394-e80247b12499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438601} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb8753c-ab98-5e82-82cd-ee42afcd9f28" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3d211e-4ac3-517a-add9-2d442df1b48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438602} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c9eb12-1057-58bb-8116-fa1399ef2296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c9eb12-1057-58bb-8116-fa1399ef2296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438603} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff0eaf1f-730a-5a51-9bf3-fa77e2cb6d36" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc1a89db-98a6-54e9-a27c-1ebc3462a7a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81460" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$\n- **II.** $G(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,i+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})$ and $Q=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=x{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371f0d39-776e-5b85-a508-c94b2ac2b522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438604} {:answer/id #uuid "aca9ec9d-b6b0-5219-90db-431dcf2f158f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "371f0d39-776e-5b85-a508-c94b2ac2b522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438604} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dda7980-1744-5da6-8443-f5cd3aa61fb8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff09ca98-e109-5e9b-a23c-9174cdfe3d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438606} {:answer/id #uuid "95604c57-6e13-5fb9-b03a-6c3250992cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95604c57-6e13-5fb9-b03a-6c3250992cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438607} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc6a548e-e667-5d89-9d10-459ef9e2e419" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063f9ca6-220f-5f24-91d4-0cff834e06d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438608} {:answer/id #uuid "bc47704e-68c1-5d9e-9f9b-802de105632e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "063f9ca6-220f-5f24-91d4-0cff834e06d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438608} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd11437-4a71-571d-b489-a706ff584c35" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2410b4fd-4172-5cce-b672-b1259e48aa6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81743" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(2x+1)\\,i+(5{y}^{2}+2y+1)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H={y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2x+1$ and $Q=5{y}^{2}+2y+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={y}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3d344f4-8ed3-593a-aed4-5228049f941e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438616} {:answer/id #uuid "b51feba4-149e-552b-ba57-a034975c6254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b51feba4-149e-552b-ba57-a034975c6254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438617} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0efaccc-6c1e-515a-a6b3-27b5fea347ab" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439337} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fceaa12c-f1cb-5a20-a916-433cb5161bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438618} {:answer/id #uuid "fdca841c-e44f-5760-937f-556b1d7dbb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fceaa12c-f1cb-5a20-a916-433cb5161bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438618} :answer-field/id #uuid "75fbead4-be61-5190-81c8-ff7df1836c09" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ce1c69-5a9a-5668-a6d1-ef1043e559b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438620} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc59867-4b98-5161-bf03-738307481109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ce1c69-5a9a-5668-a6d1-ef1043e559b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438620} :answer-field/id #uuid "521321bd-40ca-5972-9cfb-572b598d4cdd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7be69df3-7414-5358-b771-962bb47016d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81746" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=xy\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(4x+{y}^{2})\\,i-4x{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.  \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=xy$ and $Q=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=4x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e78342e-7073-5996-b8d2-29a251917883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437704} {:answer/id #uuid "1d04d934-dcdb-526f-b570-2dbcc6fce5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d04d934-dcdb-526f-b570-2dbcc6fce5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437705} :answer-field/id #uuid "b474b307-7652-5024-93ce-3d6d184d1386" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438881} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65dc72a9-ac06-5f1a-bf28-b0607daf1e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437706} {:answer/id #uuid "a136b1c8-384b-51aa-80e4-6450fc364d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65dc72a9-ac06-5f1a-bf28-b0607daf1e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437706} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c7e673-aa45-56c5-83bc-18fb8c69889f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0467504c-f5ba-5c65-b935-abce7630a7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437708} {:answer/id #uuid "fab9a31b-ee2c-52cf-8167-c9659890b4a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0467504c-f5ba-5c65-b935-abce7630a7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437708} :answer-field/id #uuid "8654b8c1-2a6a-54eb-b934-803e7b9c7856" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f81692c-bc38-55e0-9228-b9c2fd15506d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123866" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=2x\\,i-3(x+y)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i+{e}^{y-x}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{2}y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2x$ and $Q=-3(x+y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q={e}^{y-x}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{y-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "402ff24c-ce68-5100-bc6f-ce08b50c1b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437378} {:answer/id #uuid "b55bf19b-e306-5cf0-a5ee-460b10f4d0dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b55bf19b-e306-5cf0-a5ee-460b10f4d0dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437379} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0832f4-ea5d-510d-80b2-f2b601f5cea1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65c63bc1-ce38-5b41-98d2-3546373c1d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437380} {:answer/id #uuid "aadf4a2f-a9fb-5a4e-a7c3-c50425746fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c63bc1-ce38-5b41-98d2-3546373c1d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437380} :answer-field/id #uuid "245451c8-c2d8-5660-b868-914d6a09643c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3af17a-9e86-50af-944e-c33c42e3d28e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437382} {:answer/id #uuid "c5abe579-6912-52ab-ae7a-530eb7394275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5abe579-6912-52ab-ae7a-530eb7394275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437383} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd21fc02-2d66-5119-b27a-c74692938a6c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b4c4358-44ac-586a-b065-3dc8b8db940a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104574" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(8x-3y)\\,i+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519cca11-1af0-5c38-ac09-9cdf3e9eb8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439999} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbea3d5-d3a9-50cd-881a-7348e37c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fbea3d5-d3a9-50cd-881a-7348e37c0dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440000} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3485904-6c14-5182-a07e-0e8536e160b9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440357} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb20b90e-716b-5744-aa1c-1bc38cc09c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440001} {:answer/id #uuid "9e61d8e1-2d98-58e5-918c-f2fb0a227922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e61d8e1-2d98-58e5-918c-f2fb0a227922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440002} :answer-field/id #uuid "908db421-59c3-5473-9555-1804770c666a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e2896f-89e3-56ed-85e3-9569e0e553b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440003} {:answer/id #uuid "0b03afd1-5458-5b8f-80bb-6721de8fe5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b03afd1-5458-5b8f-80bb-6721de8fe5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440004} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bd3f31-bc54-5cfa-8930-506cacf26495" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "953d7cc2-ab7b-52e3-b63e-6dc18089f06f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86896" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2})\\,i+({e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=({y}^{3}-6x)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{2}$ and $Q={e}^{x+y}-\\sqrt{7}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={y}^{3}-6x$ and $Q=3x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}]}
